The stack below uses `top = 0` for an empty stack; other books use another starting value, so state your convention.

```pascal
const MaxSize = 4;
var items: array[1..MaxSize] of integer;
    top: integer;            { 0 means that the stack is empty }

procedure Push(x: integer);
begin
  if top = MaxSize then writeln('Overflow')
  else begin top := top + 1; items[top] := x end
end;

procedure Pop(var x: integer);
begin
  if top = 0 then writeln('Underflow')
  else begin x := items[top]; top := top - 1 end
end;
```
For a circular queue of size `N` with items counted in `count`, enqueue does `rear := rear mod N + 1` before storing, and dequeue does `front := front mod N + 1` after reading. The queue is full when `count = N` and empty when `count = 0`; the count is what tells a full queue from an empty one when front and rear are close. The code is an example of the logic; it was checked by an equivalent program, since no compiler for this language was available.
